data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR (clustered regularly-interspaced short palindromic repeats) was first found in bacteria, where it protects against virus invasions. [1]\n- Cas9 nuclease, delivered with a synthetic guide RNA, cuts a cell's genome at a desired location. [6]\n- After the cut, the cell may repair itself with errors that break or turn off the gene, or researchers can insert a DNA template to add a new function. [3]\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to knock out genes, and can supply repair-template DNA to replace disease-causing mutations. [2]\n- Sources 4 and 5 are Hacker News links whose titles mention treating diseases and eliminating HIV in cell culture, but they give no details. [4][5]"}

